= The Bürgergeld passes!

Since the times of Gerhard Schröder, Germany's unemployment benefits system has been the neoliberal Hartz IV. Now, as one of the most important reforms of their mandate, the <span style="color: [+ spd_color +];">new go</span> <span style="color: [+ greens_color +];">vern</span> <span style="color: [+ fdp_color +];">ment</span> has managed to pass the Bürgergeld, which increases both the amount of the monthly payment and the personal savings allowed, as well as introducing a 6-months long mild sanctions period. At the same time, the minimum wage has been increased.

As we are on the left-wing of the German political spectrum, we are expected to formulate our stance on this reform as well as the government in general.
  
What should we do?

- @criticize
- @praise


@criticize
title: Criticize the bill as being far too moderate.
on-arrival: pragmatist_dissent += 1; beweli_dissent -= 2; beweli_strength += 2; pro_status_quo -= 1; issue_welfare -= 0.5; urban_linke -= 1; east_linke -= 0.7; youth_linke -= 0.4; urban_cdu += 1; east_spd += 0.7; youth_spd += 0.4;
go-to: root

@praise
title: Praise the bill while still remarking its shortcomings.
on-arrival: pragmatist_strength += 2; beweli_dissent += 2; pro_status_quo += 1; urban_linke -= 2; east_linke -= 1; youth_linke -= 0.7; urban_spd += 1; east_spd += 1; youth_spd += 0.7; issue_welfare -= 2.5;
